# Baseline for dev/check_body_divergence.py — the alignment-vs-content audit.
#
# Format: `path_glob::symbol_glob  # reason`. Every entry MUST carry a reason; the
# script refuses to run otherwise. Use `<file>` as the symbol for a whole-file
# finding — it is not a legal Python identifier, so it cannot collide with a real
# name, and `path::*` still covers it.
#
# What this audit catches: divergence from upstream/main that is ALIGNMENT rather
# than CONTENT. That distinction is the one no line-based measure can make, and
# every wrong sizing call in docs/upstream-gaps.md was a number derived from a line
# count. `apc.py` read as 554 missing lines / 36 fork-marker sites / 75 hunks and
# was four block moves: 74 of its 76 shared definition bodies were byte-identical.
#
# Three findings, all mechanically fixable with no archaeology — which is what
# makes gating on them honest, and why this file is expected to stay near-empty:
#
#   <file>      the file differs from upstream but NOTHING in it does. Converge it.
#   ALIGNMENT   a shared definition whose bodies differ only in blank lines or
#               trailing whitespace. Converge the definition.
#   RELOCATED   a shared definition whose body is byte-identical to upstream's but
#               which sits outside upstream's definition order. Move it back.
#
# An entry here is therefore a claim that a MISALIGNMENT is deliberate, which is a
# high bar: it means upstream's ordering or whitespace is actively wrong for this
# fork. The far more likely answers are "converge it" and "it is content, so it
# belongs to check_fork_markers.py instead".
#
# THE TRAP, same one as .fork-marker-allowlist: do not excuse a finding by asserting
# it is deliberate. Three `# Fork: placement only` markers in this tree said exactly
# that — "byte-identical body ... Nothing to converge" — and all three were false;
# `git log -S` showed only upstream's own commits behind both sides of both pairs.
# See 3105b598.
#
# What this audit deliberately does NOT gate on: "every differing body must be
# reviewed". check_fork_markers.py already requires a `# Fork:` marker on each
# diverging site, and a second per-definition ledger would only add ~50 entries
# nobody had looked at — recreating the "baseline: pre-existing divergence,
# unreviewed" anti-pattern .symbol-exclusions took months to drain. Content
# divergence needs a human reading `--summary` and picking the next file.
#
# Validated to fire before being trusted: the two relocations it reported on first
# run (server/app.py::_extract_response_format_schema and
# tests/test_server.py::test_metrics_endpoint_records_chat_completion_metrics) were
# both real, and converging them took the count to zero. Reverting either commit
# makes it report again. Runs in ~3s.
#
# RESOLVED 2026-08-10, converged rather than excused (3105b598):
#   mlx_vlm/server/app.py::_extract_response_format_schema        — reordered
#   mlx_vlm/tests/test_server.py::test_metrics_endpoint_records_chat_completion_metrics
#
# Baseline: EMPTY. Keep it that way — every finding here has a mechanical fix.

